      <h1>Refer a company</h1>
      <p class="lead">Know a company with years of operating history? The data buyer pays for a qualified introduction. You don&rsquo;t need to sell anything, understand AI, or convince anyone to hand over their systems.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="#refer-form">Make an introduction</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What the data buyer pays</h2>
      <p class="reveal">If the company becomes a qualified data partner and the deal closes, you receive the greater of <strong>$50,000 or 6% of the license fees actually paid</strong> to the company, on each of your first five qualified U.S. direct referrals. Deals six to 25 pay the greater of <strong>$52,500 or 6.25%</strong>, and deals after that the greater of <strong>$55,000 or 6.5%</strong>.</p>
      <p class="reveal">For a company in Canada, the U.K. or Australia, a direct referral pays <strong>6% of the license fees with no minimum</strong>. If someone you introduced goes on to introduce another qualifying company, you earn the greater of <strong>$5,000 or 1%</strong> on your first five of those, $7,500 or 1.25% on the next twenty, and $10,000 or 1.5% after that.</p>
      <p class="muted small-note reveal">These are the data buyer&rsquo;s published terms, and they are worth reading closely. The percentage is calculated on license fees paid to the company, excluding taxes and reimbursements, and the reward is paid after the buyer has been paid. <strong>Only a full-corpus license earns a fee</strong> &mdash; pilots, samples and partial datasets do not. A referral is registered to you in writing, the agreement has to be signed within six months, and a fee can be clawed back if the company refunds the buyer or the referral breaks the program rules.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Who the data buyer is looking for</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>50+ full-time staff</strong>15+ for accounting, medical and finance firms, 10+ for law firms. The company-data programme itself has lower floors than the referral programme does.</li>
        <li><strong>3+ years of operating history</strong>With those years of records still in the company&rsquo;s systems.</li>
        <li><strong>At least five systems</strong>Email, internal chat, file storage, CRM, plus one more such as finance, calls or operations.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">The company must be based in the U.S., Canada, the U.K. or Australia, and must own the records in those systems. The fee applies to companies you introduce directly. A company introduced by someone you brought in is a secondary referral, paid at 1% or $5,000, whichever is greater.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

